  "summary": "Found a high-severity security issue where a revoked session can still bind the MCP identity. · The change introduces a security vulnerability where a revoked session can mint a new connector grant, allowing unauthorized access to the MCP bearer identity and associated resources. · The change introduces a new middleware `requireLoginSession` to ensure that only live, unrevoked login sessions can mint connector grants. However, the GET handler of `/authorize` in `bridge.js` still reads `req.session.userId` directly without using this middleware, allowing revoked sessions to complete the authorization flow. · The change introduces a security vulnerability where a revoked session can still mint a new grant, allowing unauthorized access. · The change introduces a security vulnerability where a revoked session can still complete the connector grant, allowing unauthorized access to the workspace through `/mcp`. · The change introduces tests for various scenarios related to OAuth authorization and token issuance. However, it does not address the security issue where a revoked session can still bind the MCP identity and gain full access. · Found one high-severity defect related to session revocation and authorization.",
